00:05:05.570 --> 00:05:08.561
I said, "I can't stand this anymore."

00:05:08.561 --> 00:05:12.219
I started screaming, I think,
and I feel bad for the doctor

00:05:12.219 --> 00:05:15.401
but I was like, "Hey, this can't continue.

00:05:15.401 --> 00:05:17.332
"I'm losing my mind."

00:05:17.332 --> 00:05:19.236
It's a terrible feeling to feel

00:05:19.236 --> 00:05:21.846
like you know that
something's wrong with you

00:05:21.846 --> 00:05:23.436
and the professional
who's assigned to help you

00:05:23.436 --> 00:05:24.427
doesn't believe you.

00:05:24.427 --> 00:05:26.520
I started questioning myself.

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:28.129
"Maybe the pain's in your head."

00:05:28.129 --> 00:05:31.463
What a terrible thing, to
doubt your own reality.

00:05:31.463 --> 00:05:34.141
The story after this gets pretty quick.

00:05:34.141 --> 00:05:35.748
I got referred to a rheumatologist

00:05:35.748 --> 00:05:38.240
and within minutes, the
rheumatologist is like,

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:40.156
"Oh, yeah, I think I know
what's wrong with you."

00:05:40.156 --> 00:05:41.321
And I'm like, "What?

00:05:41.321 --> 00:05:43.239
"What? That's, what
are you talking about?"

00:05:43.239 --> 00:05:45.843
I had to go in for an
MRI, which was scary.

00:05:45.843 --> 00:05:47.708
'Cause at that point I didn't
know what was wrong with me.

00:05:47.708 --> 00:05:49.306
He just referred me to
an MRI and I'm like,

00:05:49.306 --> 00:05:51.972
"Oh, God, what is this, it's terrifying."

00:05:51.972 --> 00:05:55.062
If you've never had an MRI,
they tape your legs together

00:05:55.062 --> 00:05:56.391
so you can't move.

00:05:56.391 --> 00:05:57.881
So I'm in the parking lot and I can

00:05:57.881 --> 00:06:00.913
hear the MRI machine above me.

00:06:00.913 --> 00:06:03.072
This is the sound I was listening to

00:06:03.072 --> 00:06:04.283
for the past 20 minutes.

00:06:04.283 --> 00:06:05.116
Listen.

00:06:05.116 --> 00:06:07.621
(electronic beeping)

00:06:07.621 --> 00:06:08.828
It's really scary.

00:06:08.828 --> 00:06:11.645
I'm staring at what,
I guess, is a speaker.

00:06:11.645 --> 00:06:13.953
For the life of me, it just
looks like a shower head.

00:06:13.953 --> 00:06:17.374
About this big and it's got the
little dots going around it.

00:06:17.374 --> 00:06:19.734
And you're staring at
it, the buzzing starts.

00:06:19.734 --> 00:06:21.425
And it's just repetitive, like

00:06:21.425 --> 00:06:24.508
(mimics MRI machine)

00:06:25.757 --> 00:06:27.574
And there's a person walking by me,

00:06:27.574 --> 00:06:28.665
I feel weird.

00:06:28.665 --> 00:06:29.981
I'm not used to vlogging,

00:06:29.981 --> 00:06:33.054
I feel like a crazy person doing this.

00:06:33.054 --> 00:06:34.580
No, he's riding a bike.

00:06:34.580 --> 00:06:36.013
Good for him.

00:06:36.013 --> 00:06:40.394
It turns out I have
ankylosing spondylitis.

00:06:40.394 --> 00:06:43.962
It is a real mouthful
and super fun to say.

00:06:43.962 --> 00:06:45.371
Ankylosing spondylitis

00:06:45.371 --> 00:06:47.823
affects under one percent
of the population.

00:06:47.823 --> 00:06:51.999
As I understand it, basically,
my body attacks the ligaments

00:06:51.999 --> 00:06:53.068
in between my bones.

00:06:53.068 --> 00:06:55.516
And then the body goes,
"Ooh, I know, I can fix that!

00:06:55.516 --> 00:06:56.789
"I'll put bone there."

00:06:56.789 --> 00:07:01.048
But bone is not supposed
to grow where bone isn't.

00:07:01.048 --> 00:07:04.408
So I have bone growing where
there shouldn't be bone.

00:07:04.408 --> 00:07:07.969
That causes a lot of inflammation
that causes a lot of pain.

00:07:07.969 --> 00:07:10.500
My hips had begun to fuse together.

00:07:10.500 --> 00:07:14.734
In between the gaps of my
hips, there is bone growing.

00:07:14.734 --> 00:07:17.972
Now, getting a diagnosis
of an autoimmune disease

00:07:17.972 --> 00:07:19.359
should be

00:07:19.359 --> 00:07:21.159
one of the worst days of my life, right?

00:07:21.159 --> 00:07:25.326
But, and I say this, honestly,
I was so fucking relieved.

00:07:26.627 --> 00:07:29.264
I really was, I mean, I
wanted to cry of happiness

00:07:29.264 --> 00:07:32.623
because getting that diagnosis
was the difference between

00:07:32.623 --> 00:07:35.294
being able to continue my life and not.

00:07:35.294 --> 00:07:37.137
This was about four months ago.

00:07:37.137 --> 00:07:40.658
I am happy to report that since that time,

00:07:40.658 --> 00:07:44.055
I've been on medication
and I have felt better

00:07:44.055 --> 00:07:47.393
than I have felt since childhood.

00:07:47.393 --> 00:07:48.879
I had no idea.

00:07:48.879 --> 00:07:51.351
